## Source progress — bounded native HCI frame timeout and faulted-poll gate

The native interrupt/bulk-IN pump now distinguishes an ordinary idle timeout
from the end of a five-second poll that left an earlier HCI Event or ACL frame
partly assembled. The latter faults the transport instead of retaining its
stale prefix indefinitely and concatenating future controller bytes to it.
The physical owner also refuses subsequent USB polls after the HCI stream
has faulted or detached, pending a new transport attachment/owner epoch.

`classifyUsbHciRead` retains ordinary zero-byte idle polls when no frame is
pending; a completed short read can still append the remainder of a split
frame. A synthetic C++14/C++17 host fixture covers incomplete-frame timeouts,
valid segmented transfers, owner revocation, short/ambiguous timeouts and idle
behavior. This is **source-only** USB transport recovery, not native macOS
Bluetooth host registration, a successful physical re-enumeration, BLE pairing
or AirDrop availability. Firmware identity and native host integration remain
open requirements.

## Source progress — native USB descriptor-completion error propagation

Both native USB bulk OUT writes and interrupt/bulk IN polls now validate
`IOMemoryDescriptor::complete()` after the blocking USB transfer. If the
mapping cleanup fails, the native consumer no longer treats successful pipe
I/O as a complete packet or firmware write, and an incomplete cleanup on
an idle timeout is not reported as benign `Idle`. A failed OUT completion
clears its reported successful-byte count; callers fault the transport or
abort the uncertain firmware update. Offline synthetic HCI read classification,
USB transport tests, native SDK syntax and strict source checks cover the
policy; there is still no physical USB result. Verified endpoint quiescence,
provider detach and independent native Bluetooth-host registration remain
required for physical memory-lifetime and recovery guarantees.

## Source progress — pre-credit HCI completion event length validation

Even before Read Buffer Size establishes ACL credits, Number Of Completed
Packets events now validate their declared per-handle count and exact frame
length. Previously an internally inconsistent but outer-length-valid event
was forwarded directly to the native Bluetooth host because the controller
pool was not yet configured. A synthetic regression reproduces that failure
before the fix, then confirms valid empty pre-credit completions remain
accepted while malformed completions fault transport without host delivery.
This changes neither controller authorization nor native Bluetooth host
registration; source-only tests remain the available evidence.

## Source audit — operational HCI owner still unwired

The native `openNativeUsbHciIo`, `sendNativeUsbHciCommand`,
`sendNativeUsbHciAcl`, `pumpNativeUsbHciEvent` and `pumpNativeUsbHciAcl`
entrypoints are defined in `src/platform/bluetooth/NativeAth3kIo.cpp`
(and declared in its header), but the production source tree contains no
caller that opens or drives this post-firmware HCI transport. Conversely,
`SimpleATHUsbBluetooth::probe` and `start` deliberately accept only the
bootloader-revision USB identity and release the firmware owner before
normal-mode re-enumeration; this is **not** an operational macOS Bluetooth
host-controller service. A future implementation must introduce a separately
verified post-reset USB interface owner, exclusive per-epoch endpoint lease,
HCI command/event delivery into the real macOS Bluetooth host, bounded poll
lifetime and teardown. Do not attach the old firmware loader to an already
operational HCI interface or claim BLE pairing, AirDrop or Continuity ready
from firmware bootstrap success alone. This is an audited missing wiring
contract, not a completed host integration or device test.

## Source progress — distinct post-firmware native macOS HCI owner

The AR3012 bootloader personality still accepts only physical boot-mode
revisions and retains its independent DFU firmware/upload/reset lifecycle.
The new `SimpleATHUsbHciTransport` personality matches seven published
AR3012 HCI vendor families on USB interface 0, class E0/01/01. Its physical
probe additionally checks the full published VID/PID, operational bcdDevice,
live USB provider identity and descriptor class. Bootloader devices are
rejected rather than reset or claimed by the HCI service.

The service subclasses Apple's
`IOBluetoothHostControllerUSBTransport` and forwards real start, USB HCI
event/ACL/SCO transport, host registration, power/teardown to the native
Bluetooth-family implementation instead of creating a second USB pipe owner.
A post-start identity/provider replacement closes the new base session and
fails the attach. The required native BluetoothFamily library dependency
is declared. Only the normal-mode service uses the Apple host path; the
pre-firmware owner stays isolated. Pinned MacKernelSDK headers describe this
native transport class, **but it is not a verified macOS Tahoe 25G332
export or behavioral contract**. Native Apple build, actual loaded symbol
resolution, Bluetooth pairing, LE/AirDrop and coexistence remain unverified.
The source-only checks cannot establish physical Bluetooth readiness.

## Source progress — LE advertising command callback ownership

The portable legacy LE advertising producer now snapshots its advertising
generation and host owner epoch before each asynchronous/reentrant command
submission. A synchronous USB control callback can complete the old HCI
command, revoke the advertising producer, and start a replacement session
on the **same physical USB epoch and pending advertising phase**.
The obsolete invocation must return `OwnershipLost` rather than validating
the successor's permissions or publishing a false old-operation success.
A failed old command likewise cannot fault a newly established session.
Genuine inline disable confirmation still retires the old producer to Idle
and returns success. The bounded synthetic USB fixture first reproduced
a nonzero failure with the previous implementation, then covered
replacement during begin, controller step advancement and disable.
These C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan tests and scoped strict Clang-Tidy/format
checks provide source-only evidence, not live Bluetooth advertising,
AirDrop pairing or Tahoe HCI ABI verification.

## Source progress — LE discovery USB session identity

The portable LE scan producer now binds each HCI scan procedure to the USB
transport's monotonic attach generation, not just the native owner epoch.
Passive and active start verify that the original controller survives host
permission/privacy callbacks before sending LE scan parameters. Later HCI
command completions, stop/disable and advertising-report delivery reject a
same-epoch detach/reattach rather than acting on the replacement controller.
A new explicit revoke followed by a fresh authorized scan remains possible.

The offline `test_bluetooth_le.cpp` fixture reproduces the previously
accepted same-epoch preflight rebind and checks active authorization,
pending parameter completion, scanning reports, disable and USB command
callback replacement. This is host-side state-machine evidence only: it
neither opens the physical USB endpoint nor verifies Tahoe Bluetooth host,
coexistence, LE scanning or AirDrop behavior.

## Source progress — native USB HCI IN poll session binding

The post-firmware raw HCI adapter's bounded interrupt/ACL IN poll now records
its original USB attach generation, endpoint topology, physical provider and
borrowed pipes before any permission check or descriptor preparation. Each
poll checks that original lease after the initial policy callback, descriptor
preparation and completed USB read; old bytes and errors are rejected as
`OwnershipLost` instead of feeding or faulting a successor attached under
the same owner epoch. A local portable classification helper combines the
exact issued/current session comparison with the existing USB read outcome
policy, and synthetic C++14/C++17 fixtures cover successful stale reads,
stale error/timeouts and current-owner read/partial-timeout behavior.

This change addresses the standalone native raw-HCI transport contract; the
normal macOS Bluetooth host personality retains its *separate* superclass
USB ownership and does not borrow these endpoint pipes. No real USB I/O,
Tahoe host-controller attach, BLE pairing, or AirDrop is demonstrated.

## Source progress — MCI fault-scoped GPM cursor retirement

The masked MCI RAW/receive-message polling stage now revokes the attached
GPM consumer on a physical MCI fault, mismatched staged registers, unreadable
status, ambiguous W1C acknowledgment, or lost MMIO ownership. A subsequent
coexistence query cannot borrow that retired cursor, even if a caller ignored
the reset obligation. Each callback rechecks the original epoch/index before
issuing the next acknowledgement or publishing status; a changed cursor is
not overwritten. The native owner must still serialize same-index ABA
reattachment and reset the physical MCI session when required. Host-only
synthetic register tests cover faults, failed acknowledgments, forged enable
state and synchronous cursor replacement. No native MCI IRQ worker,
coherent-DMA consumer, Bluetooth handshake or RF authorization is wired by
this step; the production implementation task remains open.

## Source progress — bounded GPM batch after masked IRQ

The portable MCI path now carries a same-epoch identity on successfully
acknowledged RAW interrupt snapshots and can collect the currently pending
16-slot coherent GPM ring into one owned, bounded diagnostic message batch.
The collection stage requires an acknowledged nonfaulted GPM notification,
checks disjoint output/DMA storage and the live cursor, skips recycled slots,
and advances the consumer only after the entire snapshot was copied. Rejected
calls preserve both cursor and output. Tests model a physical masked-IRQ poll,
wrapped ring, reserved slots, stale notifications, failed validation and
partial-data rollback with C++ sanitizers. This software stage is **not**
called by a native MCI IRQ/DMA worker and does not synchronize device DMA,
prevent a real producer full-wrap, negotiate Bluetooth coexistence, or turn on
Wi-Fi/AirDrop. A copied IRQ result alone never authorizes radio readiness.
